WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.758
In part one, this matrix A turned the unit

2
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.525
circle into an ellipse: rotate, stretch

3
00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:11.260
rotate.

4
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.900
Today we cut A into pieces

5
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.359
one for each singular value

6
00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:17.970
the biggest first.

7
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.892
If you keep only the first piece

8
00:00:20.892 --> 00:00:23.075
what happens to the circle?

9
00:00:23.075 --> 00:00:24.400
Pause and guess.

10
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.706
Multiply U Sigma V transpose out

11
00:00:31.706 --> 00:00:33.739
column times row.

12
00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:38.369
You get sigma one u one v one transpose

13
00:00:38.369 --> 00:00:42.660
plus sigma two u two v two transpose.

14
00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.898
Here is the answer to the question.

15
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.362
The first piece squeezes the whole circle

16
00:00:49.445 --> 00:00:51.838
onto one segment along u one

17
00:00:51.838 --> 00:00:53.570
four units each way.

18
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.805
The second piece squeezes it onto

19
00:00:56.892 --> 00:00:59.340
a short segment along u two

20
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.001
one unit each way.

21
00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:03.361
Each piece sends the plane

22
00:01:03.448 --> 00:01:07.120
onto a single line: each one has rank one.

23
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.237
Now add the pieces.

24
00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:12.473
Take the point one, zero on the circle.

25
00:01:12.473 --> 00:01:15.952
The first piece sends it to one point six

26
00:01:15.952 --> 00:01:18.460
zero point eight, along u one.

27
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.746
The second piece adds zero point four

28
00:01:21.746 --> 00:01:24.605
minus zero point eight, along u two.

29
00:01:24.605 --> 00:01:27.463
Tip to tail, they land at two, zero

30
00:01:27.463 --> 00:01:30.090
exactly where A sends that point.

31
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.867
The same for zero, one: three point two

32
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:37.465
one point six, plus minus zero point two

33
00:01:37.465 --> 00:01:40.721
zero point four, lands at three, two.

34
00:01:40.721 --> 00:01:43.120
Those are the columns of A.

35
00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:47.103
Every point of the ellipse is a first-piece

36
00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:49.722
step plus a second-piece step.

37
00:01:49.722 --> 00:01:52.426
How much does each piece carry?

38
00:01:52.426 --> 00:01:56.228
The squares of A's entries add to seventeen

39
00:01:56.312 --> 00:01:58.678
sixteen from the first piece

40
00:01:58.762 --> 00:02:00.790
and one from the second.

41
00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.269
The SVD also hands us the four subspaces

42
00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:07.171
with perfect bases.

43
00:02:07.171 --> 00:02:10.309
Take this three by two matrix B.

44
00:02:10.309 --> 00:02:13.827
Every row is a multiple of one, two

45
00:02:13.827 --> 00:02:15.729
so the rank is one

46
00:02:15.729 --> 00:02:19.343
and there is a single singular value

47
00:02:19.343 --> 00:02:21.530
three times root five.

48
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.244
In the input plane, v one points along one

49
00:02:26.244 --> 00:02:29.187
two: it spans the row space.

50
00:02:29.187 --> 00:02:32.435
B sends it along one, two, two

51
00:02:32.435 --> 00:02:35.480
which spans the column space.

52
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.186
v two, at a right angle

53
00:02:38.186 --> 00:02:42.210
spans the nullspace: B sends it to zero.

54
00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:46.430
The remaining u's span the left nullspace.

55
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.988
In code, one slip is very common.

56
00:02:49.988 --> 00:02:53.146
numpy's svd returns three outputs

57
00:02:53.237 --> 00:02:55.944
and people read the columns of

58
00:02:56.034 --> 00:02:58.290
the third one as the v's.

59
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.740
But that output is V transpose

60
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.240
so the v's are its rows.

61
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.840
Apply A to the first row

62
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.240
it lands on the long axis, sigma one u one.

63
00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.837
Now apply A to the first column instead.

64
00:03:14.837 --> 00:03:17.596
It lands somewhere else entirely.

65
00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:19.950
Not an SVD direction at all.

66
00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.192
Now real data, in a small table.

67
00:03:23.192 --> 00:03:25.688
Five friends rate four films

68
00:03:25.688 --> 00:03:29.265
two space adventures and two quiet dramas.

69
00:03:29.265 --> 00:03:30.596
Twenty numbers

70
00:03:30.596 --> 00:03:34.173
but only two singular values are not zero.

71
00:03:34.173 --> 00:03:36.170
The table has rank two.

72
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.437
The first v weighs every film the same

73
00:03:39.437 --> 00:03:42.280
how much a person likes films at all.

74
00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:45.646
The second has plus signs for the adventures

75
00:03:45.721 --> 00:03:48.040
and minus signs for the dramas.

76
00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.938
Give every friend two scores

77
00:03:50.938 --> 00:03:54.891
one for each taste, and draw them as points.

78
00:03:54.891 --> 00:03:57.351
Across: likes films at all.

79
00:03:57.351 --> 00:03:59.810
Up: adventure versus drama.

80
00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.709
Ana sits high, an adventure fan.

81
00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:05.081
Cai sits low, a drama fan.

82
00:04:05.081 --> 00:04:08.771
Ben likes everything, on the middle line

83
00:04:08.771 --> 00:04:11.670
and Dev and Eli fall in between.

84
00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.987
Nobody told the SVD which

85
00:04:14.065 --> 00:04:15.717
films are adventures.

86
00:04:15.717 --> 00:04:19.100
It found the split from the numbers alone.

87
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.943
Add a sixth friend who gives

88
00:04:22.024 --> 00:04:23.479
every film a four.

89
00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:26.631
That is a multiple of the first taste

90
00:04:26.631 --> 00:04:29.460
the point sits on the middle line

91
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.400
and the rank stays two.

92
00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.200
But a friend who gives Star Chase

93
00:04:34.275 --> 00:04:36.799
a five and everything else zero is

94
00:04:36.873 --> 00:04:38.953
not a mix of the two tastes.

95
00:04:39.027 --> 00:04:40.957
Part of those ratings does

96
00:04:41.032 --> 00:04:42.517
not fit on this map

97
00:04:42.517 --> 00:04:44.670
and the rank jumps to three.

98
00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.883
Last, a habit for working with AI.

99
00:04:47.883 --> 00:04:51.289
Suppose an assistant says: A is triangular

100
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.380
so its singular values are

101
00:04:53.457 --> 00:04:56.090
its diagonal entries, two and two.

102
00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:59.920
Test it with one unit vector, along one

103
00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:00.355
two.

104
00:05:00.355 --> 00:05:03.052
A stretches it to length four

105
00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:06.880
past anything a stretch of two could reach.

106
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.360
So sigma one is at least four, not two.

107
00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:14.109
So A is a sum of rank one pieces

108
00:05:14.109 --> 00:05:17.279
sigma times u times v transpose

109
00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:21.216
each flattening the plane onto one line

110
00:05:21.216 --> 00:05:23.041
the biggest first.

111
00:05:23.041 --> 00:05:26.978
The v's and u's give the four subspaces

112
00:05:27.074 --> 00:05:29.667
and a few large sigmas mean

113
00:05:29.763 --> 00:05:31.780
a few hidden factors.

114
00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.195
Next lecture

115
00:05:33.195 --> 00:05:35.816
keep only the first few pieces

116
00:05:35.898 --> 00:05:38.355
to compress a picture and find

117
00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:40.730
the main directions in data.
